 "narration": [
  {
   "id": "N01",
   "start": 0.6,
   "end": 7.333333333333333,
   "text": "A Japanese plane is pulled toward you. A thin shaving curls up out of the block."
  },
  {
   "id": "N02",
   "start": 7.733333333333333,
   "end": 17.166666666666668,
   "text": "This time, the rest of the cutting kit: chisels, the hammer, and the plane, each one beside its Western cousin."
  },
  {
   "id": "N03",
   "start": 23.966666666666665,
   "end": 34.96666666666667,
   "text": "Japanese chisels, nomi, come in two families. Tataki-nomi are struck with a hammer. Tsuki-nomi are pushed by hand, with no hammer."
  },
  {
   "id": "N04",
   "start": 35.36666666666667,
   "end": 44.166666666666664,
   "text": "A struck chisel has an iron hoop fitted around the end of its handle. This hoop is called the katsura."
  },
  {
   "id": "N05",
   "start": 48.36666666666667,
   "end": 65.36666666666666,
   "text": "The general bench chisel, struck with a hammer, is the oire-nomi. You'll also see it read oiire, or written ōire. For deep mortises there is the mukōmachi-nomi, with a thick blade."
  },
  {
   "id": "N06",
   "start": 69.56666666666666,
   "end": 103.46666666666667,
   "text": "Now look at the blade itself. A woodworking chisel is bevelled on one side only. And it's made of two layers: a thin layer of hard steel, hagane, forge-welded to a thicker body of softer iron, jigane. The softer iron supports the brittle steel and absorbs shock, so the steel doesn't break. One Japanese blade maker puts it simply: foreign blades are usually one piece of steel; Japanese blades are laminated."
  },
  {
   "id": "N07",
   "start": 103.86666666666666,
   "end": 117.8,
   "text": "The back of the blade has a shallow hollow, the urasuki. It makes the back easier to flatten. The hollow is in the back, not the bevel: the chisel is not hollow-ground."
  },
  {
   "id": "N08",
   "start": 122.0,
   "end": 129.5,
   "text": "The hammer is the gennō. One face is flat; the other is slightly domed."
  },
  {
   "id": "N09",
   "start": 129.9,
   "end": 143.96666666666667,
   "text": "Driving a nail, you strike with the flat face, then give the last blows with the domed face, so the surface isn't marked. The domed face is the kigoroshi-men: literally, the wood-killing face."
  },
  {
   "id": "N10",
   "start": 147.66666666666666,
   "end": 155.33333333333334,
   "text": "The plane is the kanna. Like the saw, it's pulled toward you. Western and Chinese planes are pushed."
  },
  {
   "id": "N11",
   "start": 155.73333333333332,
   "end": 175.23333333333332,
   "text": "The blade sits in a wooden block, the dai, usually of oak. In the national skills-test manual, the blade is set by tapping with a hammer: on the blade's head to set it deeper, and on the corners of the block's head end to draw it back."
  },
  {
   "id": "N12",
   "start": 177.63333333333333,
   "end": 194.73333333333332,
   "text": "A two-blade plane, nimai-ganna, adds a second iron behind the blade: the chip breaker, uragane. It helps stop tear-out. Like the ryōba, it is a Meiji-era development, not an ancient one."
  },
  {
   "id": "N13",
   "start": 198.93333333333334,
   "end": 212.8,
   "text": "If you work with Western tools, the jobs are the same: bench chisels, a hammer or mallet, a bench plane. The plane is the clearest difference: a Western plane is pushed."
  },
  {
   "id": "N14",
   "start": 215.6,
   "end": 232.96666666666667,
   "text": "Chisels and planes depend on a sharp edge. The skills-test manual warns that a dull tool invites injury, and never to point a chisel's edge at yourself or anyone else. It also says to sharpen chisels and planes well after use."
  },
  {
   "id": "N15",
   "start": 235.36666666666667,
   "end": 240.33333333333334,
   "text": "Next time, our first joint: the koshikake ari-tsugi."
  },
  {
   "id": "N16",
   "start": 241.33333333333334,
   "end": 251.96666666666667,
   "text": "Strike the chisel, or push it. Tap the blade into its block. Pull the plane. And keep every edge sharp."
  },
  {
   "id": "N17",
   "start": 261.8666666666667,
   "end": 271.8666666666667,
   "text": "One note: sharp-tool technique, and how to use these tools safely, is best learned from a teacher, at a bench, in person."
  }
 ],
